<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Abkhazia</h1><p class="st-meta">1463 CE – 1864 CE (approx.) · capital Sukhumi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A principality on the Black Sea coast ruled by the Shervashidze family, between Georgian, Ottoman and Russian power, until Russia abolished it in 1864.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> broke up in the 1460s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-shervashidze">Shervashidze</a>, or Chachba, family who governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhaz</a> coast as eristavis under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dadiani">Dadiani of Mingrelia</a> made themselves independent princes with their seat at Sukhumi and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lykhny">Lykhny</a>. Family tradition traced the line to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshahs</a>, a claim that cannot be verified.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princes ruled a society of nobles and peasants living by livestock, farming and coastal trade, including a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> with Ottoman ports. From the 16th century Ottoman influence grew: the coast came under Ottoman garrisons, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhaz</a> nobles adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keleshbey-chachba">Keleshbey Chachba</a> strengthened the principality but was assassinated in 1808.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His son Giorgi accepted a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1810. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the principality in 1864, and after revolts many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhaz</a> were deported to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the exodus called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahajirstvo">Mahajirstvo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-abkhazia.jpg" alt="Principality of Abkhazia" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: George Woolworth Colton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colton,_G.W._Turkey_In_Asia_And_The_Caucasian_Provinces_Of_Russia._1856_(BA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1463 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sukhumi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Keleshbey Chachba, Giorgi Shervashidze, Mikheil Shervashidze</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity and Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Abkhaz</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abkhazia, Shervashidze principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=principality-of-abkhazia" aria-label="Principality of Abkhazia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5660 646 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Abkhazia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5980 902L6036 930M5980 902L5709 970M5980 902L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M5709 970h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5980 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5980 902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=principality-of-abkhazia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
